INT. ORBITAL ARCHIVE STATION, READING ROOM - CONTINUOUS CYCLE

A quiet room of brass and glass, the curve of a viewport showing slow clouds of a gas giant. Rows of reading carrels. Archival drawers set into the walls like ship's lockers. A brass clock ticks, though no tide exists here to measure.

ARCHIVIST PELAGIA ONWUACHI-VANDERMEER (60s, gray tunic, white gloves) shelves a folio. At a carrel, a VISITOR (40s, unmarked jumpsuit, a leather satchel, polite eyes) turns the pages of a bound volume with the care of one afraid to wake it.

At another carrel sits a YOUNG SCHOLAR, THEODOSIA AMARI-KOWALSKI (20s, ink-stained fingers, tangled hair) taking notes. Across the aisle, a LIBRARY AUTOMATON, a squat brass construct with a single lens, rolls on silent wheels.

PELAGIA
Visitor. You've been in that volume for three days.

VISITOR
It is a long volume.

PELAGIA
It's forty pages.

VISITOR
They are dense pages.

PELAGIA
May I ask what you are looking for?

VISITOR
A name.

PELAGIA
Whose?

VISITOR
My grandfather's.

Pelagia sets down her folio. Adjusts a glove.

PELAGIA
He was a colonist?

VISITOR
He was a cartographer. On the survey ship Meridian.

PELAGIA
The Meridian was lost.

VISITOR
Yes.

PELAGIA
Eighty years ago.

VISITOR
Eighty-one.

The Automaton rolls to a stop beside the Visitor. Its lens irises.

AUTOMATON
Query. Do you require assistance?

VISITOR
No, thank you.

AUTOMATON
Query. Is the volume satisfactory?

VISITOR
It is.

AUTOMATON
Noted.

It rolls away. Theodosia lifts her head and watches it go.

THEODOSIA
(whispering)
It's never done that before.

PELAGIA
It's learning.

THEODOSIA
That's what worries me.

The Visitor turns another page. Stops. His finger rests on a line of tiny script. He doesn't speak. Pelagia crosses to him, quiet, and reads over his shoulder.

PELAGIA
Ah.

VISITOR
Yes.

PELAGIA
"Cartographer Imre Teague-Oyelaran. Last known position: sector nine."

VISITOR
Last known.

PELAGIA
Last known is not final.

VISITOR
No. But it is as far as the records go.

Theodosia rises from her carrel and joins them. The three of them look at the page: the Archivist, the Visitor, the scholar.

THEODOSIA
Sector nine is where the Reach begins.

VISITOR
I know.

THEODOSIA
Nobody goes into the Reach.

VISITOR
My grandfather did.

PELAGIA
(together)
Perhaps you should wait for the next survey.

THEODOSIA ^
(together)
Perhaps you should wait for the next survey.

They glance at each other. The Visitor smiles faintly.

VISITOR
I have waited eighty-one years.

PELAGIA
It is not you who waited.

VISITOR
No. But I inherited the habit.

He closes the volume gently, rests his palm on the cover, and sits there a long moment while the clock ticks and the clouds of the giant planet turn in the window.

PELAGIA
Would you like tea?

VISITOR
Please.

She goes to fetch it. Theodosia hesitates, then pulls out the chair beside him and sits.

THEODOSIA
(quietly)
I could go with you.

VISITOR
To the Reach?

THEODOSIA
To the next archive. There's a copy of the Meridian's log in Tycho Station.

VISITOR
I would like that.

THEODOSIA
Good.

The Automaton returns, bearing a tray: a pot, three cups. It sets it on the carrel.

AUTOMATON
Tea, Visitor.

VISITOR
(to the Automaton, amused)
Thank you.

AUTOMATON
You are welcome, Imre.

The Visitor goes completely still. Pelagia, at the end of the aisle, stops with her hand on a drawer. Theodosia looks from the Automaton to him.

VISITOR
(slowly)
That's not my name.

AUTOMATON
It is in the log.
